TITLE:: PltMap
summary:: A mapping curve: what a control does to a value
categories:: Libraries>PlotLib
related:: Overviews/PlotLib, Classes/ControlSpec, Classes/Warp, Classes/PltTrack

DESCRIPTION::
Input across, output up. This is the plot for the question a synthesis class asks
all the time: the slider moved a quarter of the way, so what happened to the sound?

An exponential frequency spec and a linear one are indistinguishable as numbers in
a SynthDef and completely different here. At a control value of 0.25, code::\freq::
gives 112 Hz and the linear equivalent gives 5015.

Takes a link::Classes/ControlSpec::, a Symbol that names one (code::\freq::,
code::\amp::, code::\pan::), a link::Classes/Warp::, or a Function of a value from 0
to 1. Several can be overlaid and compared.

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: spec
argument:: label
Shown in the legend. Made up from the spec if left out.
argument:: title
argument:: width
argument:: height

METHOD:: function
A Function over a domain of its own rather than over 0 to 1.
code::
PltMap.function({ |midi| midi.midicps }, 24, 108, "midi to Hz").front;
::

METHOD:: points
The curve as plain data: code::[inputs, outputs]::, inputs from 0 to 1.

METHOD:: mapWith
One value through whichever kind of mapping it is. A ControlSpec, a Warp and a
Function all answer here.

METHOD:: asSpec
Resolves a Symbol to its spec and passes anything else through.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: add
Overlay another mapping, to be compared with the first. Colours come from the theme
in turn, and the legend is stacked in a corner: mappings that share a range all end
at the same value, so a label at the end of each curve lands on top of the others.

METHOD:: marker
Where the control currently sits, from 0 to 1. Drawn as a line with a dot on each
curve, and the mapped values printed in the caption.
code::
(
m = PltMap(\freq.asSpec).front;
Routine({ (0, 0.01 .. 1).do { |t| m.marker_(t); 0.05.wait } }).play(AppClock);
)
::

METHOD:: valuesAtMarker
The mapped value of every curve at the marker.

METHOD:: logOutput
A logarithmic output axis, where an exponential mapping is the straight line and a
linear one is the curve. Often the clearest way to see what a warp does.

METHOD:: curves
METHOD:: columns
METHOD:: showLabels

EXAMPLES::
code::
// the same range mapped three ways
(
m = PltMap(\freq.asSpec, "exp", "the same range, three warps");
m.add(ControlSpec(20, 20000, \lin), "lin");
m.add(ControlSpec(20, 20000, 4), "curve 4");
m.marker_(0.25).front;
)

// on a log output axis
(
PltMap(\freq.asSpec, "exp").add(ControlSpec(20, 20000, \lin), "lin")
	.logOutput_(true).marker_(0.25).front;
)

// amplitude: a linear fader against a squared one, which is what an ear expects
(
PltMap({ |t| t }, "linear", "fader laws")
	.add({ |t| t.squared }, "squared")
	.add({ |t| t.dbamp * 0 + t.lincurve(0, 1, 0, 1, -4) }, "curve -4").front;
)

// a spec of your own
PltMap(ControlSpec(0.001, 4, \exp, units: "s"), "delay time").front;
::
